# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NON-PRODUCTION environment contract.
#
# COPY THIS TO `.env.test.local` — NOT to `.env.local`.
#
# The two filenames are one keystroke apart and mean opposite things:
#
#   .env.local        PRODUCTION credentials. The app, `npm run dev` and the
#                     ops scripts read it. `scripts/reset-platform.mjs` reads
#                     it. Nothing in this file belongs there.
#   .env.test.local   The local Supabase stack. The E2E suite, `npm run seed`
#                     and `npm run test:integration` read it. They create and
#                     destroy data for a living, which is why they get their
#                     own file and their own database.
#
# `lib/env/testDatabase.ts` enforces the split mechanically — a test runner
# pointed at anything that is not a stack on this machine throws before it
# collects a single test. This file exists so the split is also obvious by
# name, because a guard you have to remember to keep is a guard that lapses.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Where the two key values come from ------------------------------------
#
#   npx supabase start
#
# prints API_URL, ANON_KEY and SERVICE_ROLE_KEY. Paste those two keys below.
#
# THEY COME FROM THAT COMMAND AND FROM NOWHERE ELSE. Never from the Supabase
# dashboard — a dashboard key is a production key, and pasting one here points
# the seed script and the E2E suite, both of which delete data by design, at
# real players. `assertTestDatabaseUrl` would catch the URL; it cannot catch a
# production key sitting next to a local URL, because that combination simply
# fails to authenticate and reads as "the stack is broken".
#
# The printed keys are the standard local demo pair: identical on every
# machine, signed by a published JWT secret, and valid only against a stack on
# this machine. They are left as placeholders here rather than inlined so that
# no committed file in this repository ever contains something shaped like a
# service-role key — the grep that enforces that (REQ-ENV-012) should not need
# an exception, and a secret scanner should not need to be taught one.

# Local stack API. `assertTestDatabaseUrl` accepts only localhost, 127.0.0.1,
# ::1 and host.docker.internal — a hosted ref here fails fast.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321

# ANON_KEY from `npx supabase start`. RLS-bound, same as in production.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=paste-local-anon-key-from-supabase-start

# SERVICE_ROLE_KEY from `npx supabase start`. Bypasses RLS on the LOCAL stack
# only. Never prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ — see the note at the bottom of this file.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=paste-local-service-role-key-from-supabase-start

# Direct Postgres connection, used by `node supabase/tests/run.mjs` to run the
# SQL assertion suites. Each suite wraps itself in `begin; … rollback;`.
SUPABASE_DB_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres

# Origin the local app is reached on. Must also be in the local stack's
# redirect allow-list (`supabase/config.toml`), or the magic-link exchange
# lands on the Site URL holding no session — silently, with no error.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Email: dry run, always, on this side ----------------------------------
#
# Anything other than off/false/0/no means DRY RUN — payloads are logged,
# never sent. Unset also means dry run, because `isDryRun()` in
# lib/email/sendEmail.ts fails toward silence.
#
# It is set explicitly here anyway. The E2E harness FORCES it on for its own
# server (`playwright.config.ts`) and refuses to start if the resolved value
# is anything else, so a stray `EMAIL_DRY_RUN=off` exported in a shell cannot
# reach the suite. The suite drives the cron routes, which fan out mail to
# every player on a game; inheriting a live-send configuration would mail the
# fixture addresses on every run.
#
# There is no non-production configuration in which this should be `off`.
EMAIL_DRY_RUN=on

# Sender identity. Never used while EMAIL_DRY_RUN is on — present so the
# payload the dry run logs is the payload production would send.
EMAIL_FROM="Hraj Fotbal <noreply@hrajfotbal.com>"

# IBAN encoded into the Czech SPD 1.0 payment QR strings. A dummy value: the
# seeded games generate QR payloads and nobody should be able to pay one.
PAYMENT_IBAN=CZ0000000000000000000000

# Shared secret gating the cron routes. The E2E suite calls them directly, so
# it needs to match whatever the specs send. Any non-empty value works locally.
CRON_SECRET=local-dev-cron-secret

# --- Deliberately absent ---------------------------------------------------
#
# RESEND_API_KEY. Non-production never sends mail, so it never needs a key,
# and a key that is present is a key that can be used by accident. If a dry
# run ever appears to have sent something, this absence is the proof it did
# not.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, or any NEXT_PUBLIC_ spelling of a
# secret. Next.js inlines every NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable into the client bundle
# at build time. A service-role key inlined there is RLS bypassed for anyone
# who opens devtools. The name is the whole security boundary; there is no
# runtime check that would catch it.
